        heading: 'How the Web Actually Works',
        body: `Before writing a single line of code, you need a clear mental model of what happens when someone types a URL into a browser and presses Enter. This sequence of events is not magic, and understanding it precisely will save you enormous amounts of confusion later.

When a user types a URL such as "https://example.com/about" into their browser, the first thing that happens is a DNS lookup. DNS — the Domain Name System — is a distributed global database that translates human-readable domain names into IP addresses. Your browser sends a query to a DNS resolver, which checks its cache and then queries a hierarchy of DNS servers until it gets the IP address associated with "example.com." This process typically completes in milliseconds but can take longer if the domain is obscure or the resolver is not caching correctly.

Once the browser has the IP address, it establishes a connection to the server at that address. For HTTPS connections, this involves a TLS handshake — a cryptographic negotiation that establishes a secure, encrypted channel between the browser and the server. The handshake involves the server presenting a certificate proving its identity, the browser verifying that certificate against a list of trusted certificate authorities, and both parties agreeing on encryption parameters. This process adds a small latency overhead but is essential for privacy and security.

With the connection established, the browser sends an HTTP request. The request specifies the method (GET for fetching a page, POST for submitting data, PUT and DELETE for other operations), the path ("/about"), and a set of headers containing metadata about the request: what content types the browser accepts, what language the user prefers, cookies stored from previous visits, and other information.

The server receives this request, processes it, and sends back an HTTP response. The response includes a status code indicating the outcome — 200 for success, 301 or 302 for redirects, 404 for not found, 500 for a server error — along with response headers and a body containing the actual content. For a typical web page, the body is HTML.

The browser receives the HTML and begins parsing it. As it parses, it discovers additional resources referenced in the HTML: stylesheets in link tags, scripts in script tags, images in img tags. It sends additional HTTP requests for each of these resources, potentially in parallel, depending on the browser's request prioritization logic. Each resource arrives and is processed: CSS is parsed and applied to the document, scripts are executed, images are decoded and displayed.

This entire sequence — from URL entry to rendered page — is the HTTP request-response cycle, and it is the fundamental mechanism underlying every web experience. Every performance optimization in web development is ultimately an attempt to make some part of this sequence faster or more efficient. Every security concern in web development is ultimately a concern about some way this sequence could be subverted or exploited. Understanding it clearly is prerequisite knowledge for everything else.`
