        heading: 'CSS: The Visual Layer',
        body: `CSS — Cascading Style Sheets — controls the visual presentation of HTML documents. It is a language of enormous breadth and occasional perversity, capable of producing sophisticated layouts, complex animations, and responsive designs that adapt to any screen size. Understanding CSS well requires understanding not just individual properties but the rules that govern how properties are determined when multiple rules could apply: the cascade.

The cascade is the set of rules CSS uses to resolve conflicts when multiple style declarations target the same element. Three factors determine which rule wins: origin (browser default styles have the lowest priority, author styles are higher, inline styles are highest), specificity (rules that target elements more precisely override rules that target them more broadly), and order (when origin and specificity are equal, the later rule wins). Understanding these three factors eliminates most of the confusion that beginners experience when their styles do not appear to be working.

Specificity is calculated based on the selectors used in a rule. Element selectors and pseudo-elements contribute 0-0-1 to the specificity score. Class selectors, attribute selectors, and pseudo-classes contribute 0-1-0. ID selectors contribute 1-0-0. Inline styles override all selector-based rules because they have a specificity of 1-0-0-0. The !important declaration overrides the cascade entirely and should be used sparingly, typically only to override third-party library styles that cannot otherwise be modified. In practice, keeping specificity low and consistent throughout a stylesheet makes the CSS far easier to maintain. Systems that rely heavily on high-specificity selectors accumulate technical debt rapidly because overriding any rule requires escalating specificity further.

The Box Model is the fundamental spatial model of CSS. Every HTML element is treated as a rectangular box with four layers: the content area (where text and images render), padding (transparent space inside the border), border (a visible or invisible perimeter), and margin (transparent space outside the border separating the element from its neighbors). The box-sizing property controls whether the width you specify refers to just the content area or includes padding and border as well. The default value, content-box, is counterintuitive: setting width:200px on an element with padding:20px and border:1px produces a box that is 242px wide in total. The value border-box, which makes width include padding and border, is so much more intuitive that virtually every professional CSS setup begins with a universal reset: *, *::before, *::after { box-sizing: border-box; }.

Layout in CSS has evolved through several generations of technique. Float-based layouts, the dominant approach for most of the 2000s and early 2010s, used the float property to take elements out of normal document flow and arrange them horizontally. Float-based layouts were fragile and difficult to maintain; they required clearfix hacks to prevent containing elements from collapsing, and vertical alignment was particularly awkward. Flexbox, introduced broadly supported around 2015, solved one-dimensional layout: distributing elements along a single axis (row or column) with control over alignment, spacing, and wrapping. CSS Grid, which gained broad support around 2017, solved two-dimensional layout: placing elements on a grid of rows and columns with precise control over their position and span. Modern CSS layout combines Flexbox and Grid: Grid for overall page structure and major component layouts, Flexbox for the internal alignment of elements within components.

Responsive design is the practice of making a single codebase adapt visually to different screen sizes rather than building separate mobile and desktop sites. The primary tool for responsive design is the media query, which applies CSS rules conditionally based on viewport width. The mobile-first approach — writing base styles for small screens and progressively adding complexity with min-width media queries as the screen gets larger — produces more maintainable code than the desktop-first alternative because base styles are simpler and the progressive enhancement from narrow to wide requires less override logic. The viewport meta tag in HTML head is essential for mobile rendering: without width=device-width, mobile browsers render the page at desktop width and scale it down, producing the tiny zoomed-out appearance that predates responsive design.`
