    intro: `JavaScript is the only programming language that runs natively in every web browser, and that fact shapes everything about its design, its ecosystem, and its use. It was created in 1995 in ten days by Brendan Eich at Netscape, which explains some of its stranger design decisions, but it has evolved substantially over thirty years into a capable, expressive language with a rich standard library and a massive ecosystem of open-source tools. Today JavaScript runs not only in browsers but in servers via Node.js, in mobile apps via React Native, in desktop applications via Electron, and in embedded systems. For web developers, however, the browser remains the primary context, and understanding JavaScript in that context — how it interacts with the DOM, how it handles asynchronous operations, and how it is loaded and executed — is the foundation of effective web development. This guide covers JavaScript from its fundamental concepts through the practical patterns that professional web developers use every day, building a working mental model rather than an inventory of syntax.`,
