        body: `Functions in JavaScript are first-class values: they can be stored in variables, passed as arguments to other functions, returned from functions, and stored in object properties. This capability is the foundation of JavaScript's expressive power. Many of JavaScript's most useful patterns — callbacks, higher-order functions, functional programming approaches, and the module pattern — rely on functions being values.

Scope determines where variables are accessible. Each function creates a new scope for variables declared with let and const. Variables declared in an outer scope are accessible in inner scopes (this is called scope chain lookup), but variables declared in an inner scope are not accessible in the outer scope. Global scope is the outermost scope; variables declared at the top level of a script (or with var anywhere) become global, accessible everywhere. In browsers, the global scope is represented by the window object. Polluting the global scope is a source of bugs and conflicts, which is why modules (which have their own scope) are preferred for modern JavaScript.

Closures are one of JavaScript's most powerful features and the source of much initial confusion. A closure is a function that retains access to the scope in which it was defined, even after that scope's execution has completed. When a function is created inside another function, the inner function closes over the outer function's variables — it captures a reference to them. Those variables remain alive as long as the inner function exists, even if the outer function has returned.

A practical example: a counter factory function creates and returns a counter object. Each time the factory is called, it creates a new count variable in its local scope and two functions — increment and getCount — that close over that variable. The returned object contains these functions. The count variable is private: it cannot be accessed directly from outside, only through the returned functions. Calling the factory twice creates two entirely independent counters, each with their own enclosed count variable. This is the module pattern: a function creates private state that is accessible only through the returned interface.

Closures in loops are a classic source of bugs for developers learning JavaScript. The common pattern: creating an array of functions inside a for loop, where each function is intended to log the loop index. Using var for the loop counter, all functions log the same final value because var is function-scoped and all functions close over the same variable. The fix: use let instead of var, which creates a new binding for each iteration of the loop, so each function closes over a distinct value.

Arrow functions, introduced in ES6, have a shorter syntax and importantly do not have their own this binding. In a regular function, this refers to the object the function was called on (or undefined in strict mode if called without an object). This behavior creates problems when passing object methods as callbacks, because the callback loses its this context. Arrow functions solve this by inheriting this from the surrounding lexical scope. This makes arrow functions the correct choice for callbacks and most function expressions in modern JavaScript.

Higher-order functions are functions that take other functions as arguments or return functions. The Array methods — map, filter, reduce, forEach, find, some, every — are all higher-order functions. Array.map(fn) creates a new array by applying fn to each element. Array.filter(fn) creates a new array containing only elements for which fn returns true. Array.reduce(fn, initialValue) applies fn to each element accumulating a single result — it is the most general of the array methods and can implement any transformation, though it is often over-used where simpler alternatives exist. These methods together form a functional programming approach to data transformation that produces more readable and composable code than imperative loops for many common operations.`
