        body: `The DOM (Document Object Model) is the tree-structured representation of an HTML document that the browser creates when it parses HTML. JavaScript interacts with web pages by reading and modifying this tree: creating elements, changing text, updating attributes, adding and removing classes, responding to events, and inserting or removing nodes. DOM manipulation is what makes web pages interactive.

document.querySelector(selector) returns the first element matching the given CSS selector, or null if none matches. document.querySelectorAll(selector) returns a NodeList of all matching elements. These are the modern, versatile selection methods. Older alternatives like getElementById, getElementsByClassName, and getElementsByTagName still work and are slightly faster for their specific use cases, but querySelector and querySelectorAll handle all selection needs with CSS selector syntax.

Reading and writing properties: element.textContent gets or sets the text content of an element and all its descendants (HTML tags stripped). element.innerHTML gets or sets the HTML content as a string — using innerHTML to insert user-provided data is a critical security vulnerability (XSS attack vector); prefer textContent for text and DOM construction methods for HTML. element.value reads and writes the current value of form inputs. element.className reads and writes the full class string. element.classList provides a cleaner interface: add(), remove(), toggle(), contains(), and replace() methods for individual class manipulation. element.getAttribute(name) and element.setAttribute(name, value) read and write arbitrary HTML attributes. element.style.property reads and writes inline CSS — use this sparingly; class-based styling with classList is usually preferable as it keeps styling in CSS rather than JavaScript.

Creating and inserting elements: document.createElement(tagName) creates a new element. element.appendChild(child) appends a child to the end of the element. element.insertBefore(newNode, referenceNode) inserts before a specific child. The newer element.append(...nodes) accepts multiple nodes and strings. element.prepend() inserts at the beginning. element.before() and element.after() insert adjacent to the element. element.remove() removes the element from the DOM. To create a complete tree of new elements efficiently: document.createDocumentFragment() creates an off-DOM container; appending multiple elements to a fragment and then inserting the fragment into the DOM causes only a single reflow, while inserting each element individually triggers a reflow for each insertion.

Event handling is the mechanism through which JavaScript responds to user actions. element.addEventListener(eventType, handler, options) registers an event handler. Common event types: "click" for mouse clicks, "keydown" and "keyup" for keyboard input, "input" for input field changes, "submit" for form submissions, "focus" and "blur" for focus changes, "mouseover" and "mouseout" for hover, "touchstart" and "touchend" for touch devices. The event object passed to the handler contains information about the event: event.target is the element that triggered the event, event.currentTarget is the element the listener is attached to, event.preventDefault() prevents the default browser behavior (e.g., preventing form submission or link navigation), event.stopPropagation() prevents the event from bubbling up to parent elements.

Event delegation is an efficient pattern for handling events on multiple similar elements. Instead of attaching a listener to each element, attach a single listener to a parent. When an event occurs, it bubbles up from the target element through its ancestors. The listener on the parent receives the event; you check event.target to identify which child triggered it. This pattern is memory-efficient (one listener instead of many), handles dynamically added elements without requiring new listeners, and makes event management simpler. A single click listener on a list that checks if event.target is an li element handles clicks on all list items past, present, and future.`
